## Introduction
High-valent transition metal-oxo species have been invoked as reactive intermediates for a wide range of oxidation reactions with both enzymatic and synthetic catalysts.[1–3] Many terminal oxo complexes are powerful oxidants and serve as promising active site motifs for the activation of strong $C-H$ bonds, such as those of light alkanes, at moderate reaction conditions.[1–5] However, terminal oxo complexes are often difficult to isolate and probe experimentally as a direct consequence of their inherent reactivity especially those containing mid-to-late transition metals.[6–9] In the synthesis of molecular metal-oxo complexes, sterically bulky ligands are typically employed to protect highly reactive metal-oxo units, as it can be difficult to prevent them from dimerizing or deactivating in solution.[10] A different strategy is to leverage the well-defined metal sites incorporated within the inorganic nodes of metal−organic frameworks (MOFs) to support reactive metaloxo species in a heterogeneous catalyst.[11] This approach takes advantage of the site isolation, uniformity, and periodicity of metal sites when present as part of the secondary building units of MOFs.[12] Enabled by the vast library of inorganic nodes and organic linkers, the highly tunable nature of MOFs also makes it possible to design active sites with tailored coordination environments for a given reaction of interest.[13,14]
Despite the many thousands of MOFs that have been synthesized to date,[15,16] the structural diversity of MOFs has hardly been explored for this challenging class of reactions. As a result, relatively little is known about how to enhance the reactive properties of these materials despite the modular nature of the underlying secondary building units.[17] To date, nearly all MOFs studied for the activation of light alkanes via terminal metal-oxo species contain metals connected to one another with carboxylate linkers that yield an all-oxido coordination environment, as is the case for the metal sites of Fe-MOF-74 (and its structural analogues),[18–22] Mn-exchanged MOF-5,[23] $M_{3}(B\mathsf{T C})_{2}$ $\mathsf{M}=\mathsf{C r}$ , Fe, Co, Ni, Cu, Zn; $\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B T C}\ =\ 1,3,4$ 5-benzenetricarboxylic acid),[24] $\mathsf{F e M}_{2}$ -PCN-250 ${\mathsf{M}}={\mathsf{F}}{\mathsf{e}}$ , Mn, Co, Ni; ${\mathsf{P C N}}=$ Porous Coordination Network),[25] and Fe-MIL-100 (MIL $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ Materials Institut Lavoisier).[26,27] Fe-BTT $(\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B T T}=1,3,5$ -benzenetristetrazolate), which has tetrazolate linkers, is one exception; however, it is believed that framework defects (rather than the computationally investigated, crystallographic framework sites) are responsible for the material’s reactivity toward ethane in the presence of $N_{2}O$ [28]
Metal−triazolate frameworks represent one family of MOFs that have not yet been widely explored for activating the strong C−H bonds of light alkanes. Like many metal−azolate frameworks (MAFs),[29] metal−triazolate frameworks with open metal sites often contain anionic ligands within the first coordination sphere of the metal, which can typically be post-synthetically exchanged.[30] This represents a promising route to alter the reactivity of a given framework, beyond the more common design handles of metal identity and linker functionalization. This is further motivated by prior studies of transition metal-oxo complexes, which have shown that the presence and identity of coordinating, anionic ligands can greatly enhance rates of hydrogen atom transfer reactions.[5,31,32] Of the previously synthesized MAFs containing open metal sites, M-BTT,[33] MBTTri $(\mathsf{H}_{3}\mathsf{B T T r i}=1,3,5.$ -tri(1H-1,2,3-triazol-5-yl)benzene),[34,35] and M-BDTriP ( $\cdot H_{3}B D\mathsf{T r i P}$ $\mathbf{\tau}=\mathbf{\tau}$ 5,5′-(5-(1H-pyrazol-4-yl)-1,3- phenylene)bis( $^{1H-1,2,3}$ -triazole))[34] all have $\mu_{4}{-}\mathsf{C l^{-}}$ ligands, ${\mathsf{M}}_{2}{\mathsf{C l}}_{2}({\mathsf{B B T A}})$ $(1+28\mathsf{B}\mathsf{T}\mathsf{A}=1H,5H\mathsf{-b}\mathsf{e n}z\mathsf{o}(1,2\mathsf{-d}:4,5\mathsf{-d}^{\prime})\mathsf{b i}$ striazole)[36] and $\mathsf{M}_{2}\mathsf{C l}_{2}(\mathsf{B T D D})$ (H2BTDD = bis(1H-1,2,3-triazolo[4,5-b],[4′,5′- i])dibenzo[1,4]dioxin))[37] have $\mu{-}C1^{-}$ ligands, and M-MFU-4l (MFU $\mathbf{\tau}=\mathbf{\tau}$ Metal−Organic Framework Ulm) has terminal ${\mathsf{C l}}^{-}$ ligands[38,39] that can potentially be exchanged. Post-synthetic ligand exchange in the aforementioned materials has led to the discovery of MOFs with enhanced water uptake,[40] $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ binding strengths,[41] hydrogen storage properties,[42] and turnover frequencies for $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ reduction[43] among several other applications.
MOFs with triazolate linkers may also be promising for stabilizing reactive metal-oxo active sites given that triazolate groups are stronger donors than the more widely used carboxylate (as well as tetrazolate) linkers.[44] In the context of
one-electron oxidation reactions and experiments involving the chemisorption of $\O_{2}$ at open metal sites, increasing the basicity of the linker – a proxy for its donor strength – has been shown to increase the redox activity of the framework.[34,41,44] Separate studies have also shown that this often serves to increase the framework’s chemical stability.[44] The greater redox activity of the framework is particularly relevant, as mechanistic studies based on density functional theory (DFT) calculations frequently indicate that the reactivity of MOFs with open metal sites are limited by their ability to form high-valent metal-oxo active species.[25,26,45]
In the present study, we focus on the family of metal−triazolate frameworks with the general formula ${\mathsf{M}}_{2}{\mathsf{X}}_{2}({\mathsf{B B T A}})$ ${\bf\ddot{M}}=$ divalent metal, $\mathsf{X}=$ monovalent bridging anion). ${\mathsf{M}}_{2}{\mathsf{X}}_{2}({\mathsf{B B T A}})$ is topologically analogous to the MOF-74 family, containing a high density of coordinatively unsaturated metal sites with square pyramidal coordination geometries and a honeycomb-like crystal structure (Figure 1). Many members of the ${\mathsf{M}}_{2}{\mathsf{X}}_{2}({\mathsf{B B T A}})$ family are relatively stable, maintaining their crystallinity up to $400~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ in a ${\sf N}_{2}$ atmosphere and withstanding exposure to ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf O}_{2}$ , $\mathsf{C l}_{2}$ , $\mathsf{B r}_{2}$ , $N H_{3}$ , and other reactive gases.[37,46,47] Perhaps most notably, like several of the aforementioned MAFs, the ${\mathsf{M}}_{2}{\mathsf{X}}_{2}({\mathsf{B B T A}})$ family is highly tunable, both with regards to the metal identity as well as the bridging monovalent anions connecting each metal center. To date, ${\mathsf{M}}_{2}{\mathsf{C l}}_{2}({\mathsf{B B T A}})$ $(\mathsf{M}=\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}$ , Fe, Co, Ni), $M_{2}(O H)_{2}(B\mathsf{B}\mathsf{T}A)$ $(M=\mathsf{C o}$ , Ni), ${\mathsf{N i}}_{2}{\mathsf{F}}_{2}$ (BBTA), Ni2Br2(BBTA), and the large-pore analogues $\mathsf{C u}_{2}\mathsf{C l}_{2}(\mathsf{B T D D})$ and $V_{2}C|_{2.8}$ (BTDD) have all been experimentally synthesized.[36,40,43,47–51] For reference, the pores of these materials are quite large, with diameters of approximately 13 Å and $23\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ for ${\mathsf{M}}_{2}{\mathsf{X}}_{2}({\mathsf{B B T A}})$ and ${\mathsf{M}}_{2}{\mathsf{X}}_{2}({\mathsf{B T D D}})$ , respectively.[52]
Using DFT, we investigate the effects of exchanging the metal cation $\langle{M}={\mathsf{V}}$ , Cr, Mn, Fe, Co, Ni, Cu) and bridging ligand $({\mathsf{X}}={\mathsf{F}}$ , Cl, Br, OH, SH, SeH) on the framework‘s ability to form reactive metal-oxo motifs for the oxofunctionalization of strong $C-H$ bonds. While some of these combinations of metals and ligands may not yield frameworks capable of forming (meta-)stable metal-oxo motifs, we include a wide range of metals and ligands to aid in the identification of overarching structure−property relationships across this family of tunable materials. As a result of this study, we show how the choice of linker and anions within the first coordination sphere can be used to increase the stability of high-valent metal-oxo sites that are reactive toward strong $C-H$ bonds in the ${\mathsf{M}}_{2}{\mathsf{X}}_{2}({\mathsf{B B T A}})$ family of metal−triazolate frameworks. We also use the ${\mathsf{M}}_{2}{\mathsf{X}}_{2}({\mathsf{B B T A}})$ family to demonstrate the important role of electron spin for $C-H$ bond activation via terminal metal-oxo species, while simultaneously clarifying several questions about the radical-like character of the metal-oxo motif.
## Results and Discussion
Throughout this work, we primarily focus on two key reactivity parameters: the thermodynamic favorability of forming a terminal metal-oxo site and the subsequent barrier for $C-H$ activation. The enthalpy of metal-oxo formation, $\Delta H_{0}$ , can be described by
$$
\Delta H_{0}=H_{\mathrm{MOF-}0}-H_{\mathrm{MOF}}-0.5H_{0_{2}}
$$
where $H_{\mathrm{MOF-O}}$ , $H_{\mathrm{MOF}}$ , and ${{H}_{{0}_{2}}}$ represent the enthalpies of the oxidized MOF, adsorbate-free MOF, and gas-phase $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ , respectively (Scheme 1a). While $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ is a natural choice for the gaseous reference state, this decision is in principle arbitrary, and all $\Delta H_{0}$ values would simply be linearly offset if another molecule (e.g. $N_{2}O$ , ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ ) were used as the reference state. As a probe for each MOF’s ability to activate strong $C-H$ bonds, we computed the barrier for the $\mathsf{H}$ -abstraction of methane (Scheme 1b). Methane was chosen due to the societal need for a catalyst that can directly convert methane to methanol at moderate reaction conditions[53–55] and its small size that permits the computational investigation of many catalyst structures. The trends in this work are expected to be generalizable to light hydrocarbons beyond methane, as $C-H$ activation barriers tend to scale with the bond dissociation enthalpies.[56] The apparent activation enthalpy for breaking the $C-H$ bond of methane is given by
$$
\Delta H_{\mathrm{a,C-H}}^{\mathrm{app.}}=H_{\mathrm{C-H}}^{\ddagger}-H_{\mathrm{MOF-0}}-H_{\mathrm{CH_{4}}}
$$
where $H_{\mathrm{C-H}}^{\ddagger}$ is the enthalpy of the transition state for H-abstraction, and $H_{\mathrm{CH_{4}}}$ is the enthalpy of gas-phase $C H_{4}$ . The apparent and intrinsic activation enthalpy, $\Delta H_{\mathrm{a,C-H}}$ , are related to one another via
$$
\Delta H_{\mathrm{a,C-H}}=\Delta H_{\mathrm{a,C-H}}^{\mathrm{app}}+\left\lvert\Delta H_{\mathrm{CH_{4}a d s.}}\right\rvert
$$
The identity of the bridging ligand can also greatly influence the thermodynamic feasibility of forming a metal-oxo species. In general, the use of OH (and to a lesser extent, SH or SeH) bridging ligands rather than the halides is recommended for the purposes of forming metal-oxo active sites (Figure 2a). After clustering the results by $X=\{F,{\mathsf{C l}},{\mathsf{B r}}\}$ or $\mathsf{X}=\{\mathsf{O H},\mathsf{S H},\mathsf{S e H}\}$ , more basic bridging ligands tend to yield metal centers with more exothermic $\Delta H_{0}$ values (e.g. $\Delta H_{\mathrm{{O,M-BBTA-F}}}<\Delta H_{\mathrm{{O,M-BBTA-Cl}}}<$ $\Delta H_{0,\mathrm{M}}$ −BBTA−Br) for the early transition metals. This is analogous to the predicted trends for $\O_{2}$ adsorption at the open metal sites of the ${\mathsf{M}}_{2}{\mathsf{X}}_{2}({\mathsf{B B T A}})$ family,[41] wherein more basic bridging ligands resulted in a greater degree of electron-donation to the metal center, increasing the thermodynamic favorability of the $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ binding process. Nonetheless, this behavior becomes less pronounced as one goes across the periodic table (Figures $26$ and 2c), with an eventual switch for the late transition metal cations (i.e. $\mathsf{M}=\mathsf{C o}$ , Ni, Cu) for which more basic ligands tend to yield less thermodynamically stable metal-oxo sites (e.g. $\Delta H_{\mathrm{{O,M-BBTA-Br}}}<\Delta H_{\mathrm{{O,M-BBTA-Cl}}}<\Delta H_{\mathrm{{O,M-BBTA-F}}}~,$ . We attribute this phenomenon to a tradeoff between the basicity of the bridging ligand (which increases as $B r<C l^{-}<F$ ) and the partial charge on the metal (which decreases as $F^{\prime}>C F>B r$ based on electronegativity trends, as shown in Figure S10a). For the early transition metals that can readily form the hypothesized $[{\mathsf{M O}}]^{2+}$ site, a ligand with a greater basicity is expected to better stabilize the increased charge on the metal. However, for the later transition metals where little or no change in charge on the metal occurs (Figure S10b), the basicity of the ligand has less of an influence than its electronegativity.
Beyond the choice of anionic bridging ligands, the donor strength of the organic linker is also likely to influence the redox activity of the framework in cases where an $[{\mathsf{M O}}]^{2+}$ site can form. To test this, we compared the $M{-}0$ formation enthalpies of the Fe-BBTA-X cluster models with those of $\mathsf{F e}_{0.1}\mathsf{M g}_{1.9}(\mathsf{D O B D C})$ $({\mathsf{H}}_{4}{\mathsf{D}}{\mathsf{O B D C}}\ =\ 2,5.$ -dihydroxybenzene- $^{1,4}$ -dicarboxylic acid) and $\mathsf{F e}_{3}(\upmu_{3}{-}\mathsf{O})(\mathsf{H C O O})_{6}$ (Figure S11), the latter of which is representative of the node in the Fe-MIL-100/101 (and Fe-PCN250) series. These MOFs were chosen as points of comparison, as they have been shown to activate strong $C-H$ bonds via a presumed Fe(IV)-oxo intermediate[18,25,26] and have carboxylate linkers, which are weaker donors than the triazolate linkers of the ${\mathsf{M}}_{2}{\mathsf{X}}_{2}({\mathsf{B B T A}})$ series.[44]
At the B3LYP-D3(BJ)/def2-TZVP level of theory, $\left[59-64\right]\Delta H_{0}$ is predicted to be $+24\mathrm{\kJ/mol}$ for $\mathsf{F e}_{0.1}\mathsf{M g}_{1.9}(\mathsf{D O B D C})$ and $+35$ kJ/mol for $\mathsf{F e}_{3}(\upmu_{3}{-}\mathsf{O})(\mathsf{H C O O})_{6}$ . While Fe-BBTA-Br has a $\Delta H_{0}$ value nearly equivalent to that of $\mathsf{F e}_{0.1}\mathsf{M g}_{1.9}(\mathsf{D O B D C})$ , all other members of the Fe-BBTA- $\boldsymbol{\cdot}\boldsymbol{\times}$ series have more favorable $\mathsf{F e}(\mathsf{N})$ - oxo formation enthalpies, with Fe-BBTA-X $({\mathsf{X}}={\mathsf{O H}}$ , SH, SeH) having $\Delta H_{0}$ values that are ${\sim}34\ \mathrm{kJ/mol}$ more exothermic than $\mathsf{F e}_{0.1}\mathsf{M g}_{1.9}(\mathsf{D O B D C})$ and ${\sim}45\ \mathrm{kJ/mol}$ more exothermic than FeMIL-100/Fe-MIL-101/Fe-PCN-250 (Table S9). This supports the hypothesis that the N donors of the more strongly donating triazolate linker are better at stabilizing the oxidized state of the metal center than the more weakly donating but commonly studied carboxylate linkers. Although the donor strength of the linker is not the only factor that can influence the metal-oxo formation enthalpy (other properties, such as coordination number and spin state are expected to play a role), the use of triazolate linkers, in comparison to the widely used carboxylate linkers, is one route to increase the thermodynamic favorability of forming terminal metal-oxo sites in MOFs with open metal sites.
Up to this point, we have simply referred to the $[{\mathsf{M O}}]^{2+}$ sites as “metal-oxo” species in line with formal oxidation state counting rules. However, the true electronic structure of these $[{\mathsf{M O}}]^{2+}$ sites is expected to exist on a spectrum with a varying degree of oxo and oxyl character (Figure 3a).[3] Several complementary methods can be used to assign a “best” resonance structure. Here, we use the partial atomic charge on the $\textsf{O}$ atom, $q_{0}$ , the spin density on the O atom, $\rho_{0}$ , and the computed $_{{\mathsf{M}}-{\mathsf{O}}}$ bond order to probe the properties of the $[{\mathsf{M O}}]^{2+}$ site. The partial atomic charge is not the same as the expected oxidation state,[65] so we have computed the partial charge on the O atoms of $H_{2}O$ and ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf O}_{2}$ as reference points for ${\mathsf{O}}^{2-}$ and $\upsigma$ oxidation states, respectively.
As shown in Figure 3b, the oxo/oxyl ligand exhibits a wide range of partial charges. In addition, the thermodynamic feasibility of forming the $[{\mathsf{M O}}]^{2+}$ site is correlated with the degree of charge transfer for a given metal identity. Based on the partial charge and spin density analysis (Figure 3c), the V-, Cr-, Mn-, and Fecontaining MAFs can be best described as having at least some degree of oxo-like character. The Co-, Ni-, and Cu-containing frameworks, however, are best described as having predominantly oxyl character. The computed $_{{\mathsf{M}}-{\mathsf{O}}}$ bond orders (Figure 3d) are in agreement with this electronic structure assignment, albeit with a clearer divide between the Cocontaining MAFs and the Ni- or Cu-containing MAFs. The computed bond orders are qualitatively consistent with what would be expected on the basis of the “oxo wall” principle.[6]
With a more thorough understanding of the $[{\mathsf{M O}}]^{2+}$ sites in the M-BBTA-X materials, we shift our focus to their reactivity, specifically with regards to activating the strong $C-H$ bond of methane, which has a bond dissociation enthalpy of $439\mathrm{~kJ/mol}$ at 298 K.[66] Excluding the V-BBTA-X series, the metal-oxo site in every MOF studied in this work has an apparent $C-H$ activation enthalpy less than $100\ \mathrm{kJ/mol}$ , with many having an apparent activation enthalpy around $50\mathrm{kJ/mol}$ or less (Figure 4). In contrast with what is typically assumed,[67] we emphasize that activating the $C-H$ bond of methane is predicted to be relatively easy in general, and efforts should instead be focused on forming the $[{\mathsf{M O}}]^{2+}$ active site. Importantly, the low activation enthalpies for breaking the $C-H$ bond of methane are not restricted to late transition metals, some of which (particularly the Ni- and Cucontaining frameworks) would be unlikely to form the pre-requisite $[{\mathsf{M O}}]^{2+}$ sites. Unlike the metal-oxo formation enthalpies in Figure 2, the differences in reactivity as a function of bridging ligands are less consistent, not showing any widespread trends except that the $\mathsf{X}=\{\mathsf{O H}$ , SH, SeH} bridging ligands in the Mn-BBTA-X series have significantly higher $C-H$ activation barriers than the halide bridging ligands.
There has been significant debate in the literature regarding the effect of spin density on the reactivity of metal-oxo complexes.[68–70] Some have claimed that localized spin density on the oxo ligand is a necessary condition for them to be reactive,[68] whereas others claim that this is not necessarily the case.[69] On the basis of the apparent $C-H$ activation barriers in Figure 4, we can immediately conclude that significant spin density on the oxo ligand is not required, as the Cr-BBTA-X series and several members of the Mn-BBTA-X series have a very low degree of spin density (Figure 3c) yet are highly reactive (Figure
4). Significant spin density on the oxo ligand at the transition state is also not necessary, as exemplified with the Fe-BBTA-X series, which has negligible spin density (Figure 5a) but an apparent activation enthalpy of $\sim50\mathrm{~kJ/mol}$ (Figure 4). These conclusions are in agreement with the perspective of Saouma and Mayer, where the authors state that, while spin density on the $0\times0$ ligand can influence the reactivity, it is not an inherent requirement.[69,71] Indeed, prior work has shown that there is often,[58,72] but not always,[57] an inverse correlation between spin density on the abstracting atom and the barrier for $C-H$ activation of the substrate. For the metal−triazolate frameworks studied here, there is not a strong correlation between the spin density on the oxo ligand and the $C-H$ activation barrier (Figure S14).
By comparing the spin density analysis before and at the transition state for $C-H$ activation, there are several catalytic candidates where the initial $[{\mathsf{M O}}]^{2+}$ state has a ferromagnetically aligned ground state, but a spin flip must occur as the transition state is approached (Figure 6). This appears unique to the Mnand Fe-containing frameworks (although the ferromagnetic and antiferromagnetic states for the $[{\mathsf{M n O}}]^{2+}$ species are often close in energy, as shown in Table S13). The relative enthalpies of various spin states for two representative MOFs, Mn-BBTA-F and Fe-BBTA-F, are illustrated in Figure 7 and indicate that this change from a parallel alignment of spins (red) to antiparallel alignment (blue) occurs in both frameworks without a change in the overall spin multiplicity of the system. We note that an antiferromagnetically aligned iron-oxo complex could only be isolated computationally if a constrained geometry optimization was performed with the Fe−O bond artificially stretched beyond that of the initial state, mimicking the increase in bond length associated with the transition state. Therefore, this spin state appears to only form along the $C-H$ activation reaction coordinate. This type of spin state behavior has previously been predicted in an $[{\mathsf{F e O}}]^{2+}$ complex, where it was concluded that a spin-coupled electron pair must form as the Fe−O bond elongates to form a reactive oxyl radical en route to the transition state.[73,76]
The apparent change from ferromagnetic to antiferromagnetic coupling represents a pronounced example of two-state reactivity (TSR),[77,78] except unlike most TSR scenarios where there is a spin crossing between two different spin multiplicities, here the spin transition occurs between two arrangements of spins for a fixed spin multiplicity. Comparing the $C-H$ activation barriers for Mn-BBTA-F along several relevant spin channels as one example, the pathway involving antiferromagnetic coupling of the unpaired electrons on the metal and the three-center C−H−O radical (Figure 7a, blue) reduces the activation enthalpy by 92 kJ/mol with respect to the ferromagnetically aligned transition state (Figure 7a, red). A similarly large activation enthalpy difference of $87\ \mathrm{kJ/mol}$ is observed for Mn-BBTA-Br (Table S11). This phenomenon is also present for the Fe-containing frameworks, as has been noted previously by Neese and coworkers.[73,76,79] For Fe-BBTA-F, the antiferromagnetically aligned transition state (Figure 7b, blue) reduces the barrier for $C-H$ activation by $51\mathrm{~kJ/mol}$ with respect to the ferromagnetically coupled transition state (Figure 7b, red). A similar $39k J/\mathsf{m o l}$ enhancement is seen for Fe-BBTA-Br (Table S12).
A population analysis at the transition state indicates that the magnitude of the spin density on the abstracting O atom is approximately equal in the ferromagnetic and antiferromagnetic high-spin states for Mn-BBTA-F (Table 1). The drastic difference in activation enthalpies is better explained within the context of exchange-enhanced reactivity[80–83] rather than by the degree of radical-like character. Exchange-enhanced reactivity implies that an increase in the number of identical-spin electrons on the transition metal center can stabilize the transition state via a greater number of exchange interactions, provided this exchange stabilization is greater than the energy required to place the electron in an otherwise vacant orbital. In the case of methane activation, this electron comes from the cleavage of the $C-H$ bond and can either be added to an empty $3d$ orbital on the metal or paired up with an electron of the opposite spin in a singly occupied molecular orbital.[82] As shown in Table 1, for both Mn-BBTA-F and Fe-BBTA-F, the pathway that increases the spin density (i.e. increases the number of spin-identical electrons and thereby exchange interactions) on the metal center is the one with the greatly reduced $C-H$ activation barrier. Most often, exchangeenhanced reactivity is used to rationalize an increase in the spin multiplicity at the transition state for $C-H$ activation. Here, however, the spin multiplicity is constant because the electron is added to the metal in combination with a spin-flip on the abstracting O atom that forms the three-center C−H−O radical.
Finally, returning to the concept of spin density on the oxo ligand, it is worth noting that if one focuses on a single spin channel in the examples shown in Figure 7, the lowest barrier corresponds to the active site with the most radical-like character on the abstracting O atom (i.e. $S=3/2$ (AFM) for Mn-BBTA-F and $S=3$ (FM) for Fe-BBTA-F, as shown in Table 1). This suggests that when attempting to rationalize the barrier heights of individual spin channels for a single catalytic candidate, spin density on the oxo ligand can be illustrative. However, the possibility of a spin transition during the $C-H$ activation process is one factor that prevents a direct relationship between the radical-like character on the oxo ligand and the activation enthalpy of the process when calculated by comparing the enthalpies of the most stable initial state and transition state.
## Conclusion
Significant attention in recent years has been focused on using MOFs with coordinatively unsaturated metal sites for the purposes of oxidation catalysis. In MOFs where a terminal metaloxo species is the proposed active site, one of the known challenges is stabilizing the fleeting nature of the metal-oxo complex, if it can even be formed at all. In this work, we studied a highly tunable family of metal−triazolate frameworks with the general formula ${\mathsf{M}}_{2}{\mathsf{X}}_{2}({\mathsf{B B T A}})$ $\langle{\sf M}=$ divalent metal, $\mathsf{X}=$ monovalent bridging anion, $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B B T A}=1H,5H\mathsf{-b e n z o}(1,2\mathsf{-d}:4,5\mathsf{-d}^{\prime})\mathsf{I}$ bistriazole). The results of the quantum-chemical investigation highlight how anion-exchange of the bridging ligands can be used to tune the reactivity of MOFs for oxidation catalysis. In particular, MOFs with $\mathsf{\Pi}\upmu\mathrm{-}\mathsf{O H}^{-}$ , $\mu{-}S H^{-}$ , and $\mu{-}\mathsf{S}{\mathsf{e}}{\mathsf{H}}^{-}$ ligands tend to be better at stabilizing the metal-oxo species than $\mu-F^{-}$ , $\vert\mu-C\vert^{-}$ , and $\mathsf{\Pi}\mathsf{\Pi}\mathsf{\Pi}\mathsf{\Pi}\mathsf{\Pi}\mathsf{\Pi}\mathsf{\Pi}$ ligands. The basicity of the bridging ligand is also qualitatively correlated with the thermodynamic favorability of forming the $[{\mathsf{M O}}]^{2+}$ site, with trends that are a function of metal identity. With regards to the reactivity of the hypothesized metal-oxo sites, relatively low (i.e. $<$ $75\mathrm{kJ/mol};$ apparent $C-H$ activation barriers are predicted for all the frameworks in this work except those containing $\mathsf{V}^{2+}$ cations. These results suggest that future efforts should primarily be focused on stabilizing the $[{\mathsf{M O}}]^{2+}$ site rather than on improving their ability to activate strong $C-H$ bonds.
For the Mn- and Fe-containing frameworks in particular, the high reactivity can be attributed to the formation of antiferromagnetically coupled spin density on the metal binding site and the abstracting O atom at the transition state for $C-H$ bond activation, which is not necessarily present in the initial $[{\mathsf{M O}}]^{2+}$ state. In cases where there is a ferromagnetic to antiferromagnetic transition en route to the transition state, this is an example of two-state reactivity, albeit one with a fixed spin multiplicity. The more reactive pathway is the one that increases the spin density on the metal center, consistent with the concept of exchange-enhanced reactivity. In addition, we note that the presence of spin density on the abstracting O atom at either the initial $[{\mathsf{M O}}]^{2+}$ state or the $C-H$ activation transition state is not an inherent requirement for low $C-H$ activation barriers. We expect that these trends are not restricted to the ${\mathsf{M}}_{2}{\mathsf{X}}_{2}({\mathsf{B B T A}})$ family of materials and may be used to rationalize the reactivities of other inorganic, bioinorganic, and enzymatic catalysts wherein terminal metal-oxo species are believed to carry out the oxidation of strong C−H bonds.
